  1. Token identity is fixed at construction from ICurvePool(pool).coins(0/1) and passed through _rewriteAltETH, which
  maps only the 0xEeee… sentinel to the internal ETH sentinel. So TOKEN_1/TOKEN_2 equal exactly what the pool reports,
  and the whole ETH-handling design of this contract keys off the sentinel: joinPoolAndStake sets msgValue only when a
  leg is the sentinel, WETH.withdraw runs only then, and unstakeAndExitPool re-wraps only when a leg is the sentinel.
  2. For the configuration this V2 branch is written against — a pool whose ETH leg reports the alt-ETH sentinel — entry
  and exit are symmetric: msgValue > 0 ⇒ use_eth = true on add_liquidity, use_eth = true on
  remove_liquidity/remove_liquidity_one_coin, native ETH received, then WETH.deposit{value: exitBalances[i]}() when
  ASSET == WETH. TokenUtils.tokenBalance on the sentinel leg measures the native balance, so the two-sided delta is
  correct. No leg is dropped and no payout bricks.
  3. The claimed divergence therefore requires a pool that both (i) is driven through the CurveInterface.V2 branch and
  (ii) reports a wrapped-ERC20 address for its ETH leg. Which interface enum a given pool is driven with, and which pool
  address is used at all, are DeploymentParams fields burned into immutables by the deployer at construction. There is
  no unprivileged call that changes CURVE_INTERFACE, CURVE_POOL, TOKEN_1, TOKEN_2, or _PRIMARY_INDEX — they are
  immutable. An ordinary depositor cannot induce the pairing; they can only redeem from a vault that the deployer
  already wired to a pool whose out-transfer semantics do not match the interface variant selected for it.
  4. The claim also rests entirely on external pool behavior that is not in the reviewed input: nothing shown
  establishes what the target pool's out-transfer does with the flag, nor that any such pairing exists. The internal
  accounting shown is self-consistent for the configuration the constructor's rewrite logic, the entry unwrap, and the
  exit re-wrap were jointly written for.

  So the failure mode described is a deployment-time interface/pool mismatch chosen by privileged configuration, not an
  unprivileged exploit path, and the code contains no unprivileged trigger that produces the asymmetry.

